Harness commit: the M1 runs used the working tree on top of commit `e93cd8a`,
with the changes listed in section 10.3; the coordinator commits them with
these results, and `versions.txt` carries the digest of the sources the binary
and the controls were built from: `789d610d...` for deep-recursion and
idle-memory, `51e42157...` (the same sources plus the fix of A1's check,
section 10.3) for max-tasks and cancel-uring, and `6a1fedf5...` (the same
harness with the machine-load record and the quiet gate of section 11, which
change only the scripts) for spawn-join, starvation, probe-overhead, echo and
idle-connections. M2's run records no commit (`versions.txt`: "{{ ver(M2, "commit") }}"),
because the workflow's memory scope passes only `PATH`, `HOME`,
`GITHUB_RUN_ID`, `JAVA_HOME` and `GOROOT` to `run_all.sh`, not `GITHUB_SHA`.
Its source digest, `{{ ver(M2, "source_sha256")[:8] }}...`, is the one M1's gated
groups were built from, so M2 ran the harness, the controls and the scripts of
those groups, which include the fix of A1's check. M3's run records its commit,
`{{ ver(M3, "commit")[:7] }}` (`versions.txt`: `{{ ver(M3, "commit") }}`), because
`.github/workflows/harness-cell.yml` passes `GITHUB_SHA` into its scope, and
the same source digest, `{{ ver(M3, "source_sha256")[:8] }}...`: the harness, the
controls and the scripts of M2's run, built by another compiler
({{ ver(M3, "rustc").split()[1] }}) into another binary.

**M2's memory scope.** M2's run executed inside `sudo systemd-run --scope -p
MemoryMax=12G -p MemorySwapMax=0` (`.github/workflows/harness-full.yml`). The
workflow's comment gives the reason: a hosted runner has no memory cgroup, and
the 1M-task cells of an earlier attempt without a cap, run 38513665561, starved
the runner agent, which lost the run; the 12 GiB scope was to leave the agent
its memory and let the harness's memory guard turn an over-large cell into a
failed row. On this runner the cap could not bind: `machine-spec.csv` records
the scope's limit, {{ num(spec(M2, "cgroup_memory_max")) }} bytes, and `MemTotal`,
{{ num(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes")) }} bytes, so the cap is
{{ gib(int(spec(M2, "cgroup_memory_max")) - int(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes")), 2) }} GiB
above the machine's memory. The guard stops a cell when the smaller of
`MemAvailable` and the cgroup's headroom falls below its margin (1 GiB in
`idle-memory`, 512 MiB in `max-tasks`), and with the cap above the machine's
memory the smaller is `MemAvailable`: the cells the guard stopped were stopped
by the size of the machine, not by the cap. They are failed rows, reported as such, all in `idle-memory` at
1M tasks: B/1 MiB at {{ created(M2, "B/1M", 1000000) }} tasks and B/8 MiB at
{{ created(M2, "B/8M", 1000000) }} (the shallow phase, the only one policy 7
requires of B at 1M), and D(B)/1 MiB at {{ created(M2, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} and
D(B)/8 MiB at {{ created(M2, "D(B)/8M", 1000000) }} of 1,000,000 (the shallow
phase stopped there, and nearly every task then skipped its excursion; the
ranges are over the five runs). No other cell met the guard: every other
`idle-memory` cell completed on M2, and `max-tasks` parked 1M tasks for every
pooled strategy. On this runner the cap therefore added no protection
beyond the guards' own margins, which are all that the lost run 38513665561 had
as well; this run completed. Two other limits of the scope shape M2's results: its
processes had an `RLIMIT_NOFILE` of {{ spec(M2, "rlimit_nofile") }}, where the runner's
own processes had {{ spec("smoke", "rlimit_nofile") }} in the smoke run (section 10.2), and
its pids cgroup allowed {{ spec(M2, "cgroup_pids_max") }} tasks (the smoke run's,
outside any scope, {{ spec("smoke", "cgroup_pids_max") }}), which stopped OS threads at
{{ med(M2, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} (section 3).

**M2's other runs.** The CI smoke run of 2026-10-13
([`results/github-runner/2026-10-13-smoke-b5349dd405e8/`](../results/github-runner/2026-10-13-smoke-b5349dd405e8/):
harness 0.1.0 at commit `b5349dd405e8`, one run per cell at smoke scale, on an
{{ spec("smoke", "cpu_model") }} with {{ spec("smoke", "cpus_available") }} vCPUs) no longer
supplies any of M2's columns or verdict figures. It is cited only for what the full run
cannot show, each labelled where it is used: the runner image's own settings,
which the workflow changes (`vm.max_map_count`, transparent huge pages, and
the descriptor and pids limits outside the scope; this section), Go under the
image's transparent huge pages `always` (section 1), the first unwind before
the unwinder fix (section 5), and C under the old 8 KiB reserve (section 1). Two full-scale
attempts before run 38518035636 were lost: run 38495423545 completed, but its
artifact was not uploaded (the artifact's name, taken from the branch, contained
a slash), and the summary captured from its job log,
[`full-38495423545-log-summary.md`](../results/github-runner/full-38495423545-log-summary.md),
is used only for the cross-check of section 10.2, not as data of record; run
38513665561, made without a memory cap, was lost as described above.

**M3's run.** Machine 3 was added after M1 and M2 had run, for one purpose: the
million-task `idle-memory` cells that M2's memory guard stopped. The workflow
`.github/workflows/harness-cell.yml` runs on `ubuntu-latest`, which for this
repository, public now, is a runner of {{ spec(M3, "cpus_available") }} vCPUs and
16 GiB (the workflow's comment; `machine-spec.csv` records
{{ num(spec(M3, "mem_total_bytes")) }} bytes of `MemTotal`), and only when a push's
head commit message carries `[harness-cell]`. Commit `{{ ver(M3, "commit")[:7] }}`
carried it, and run 41548553538 prepared the runner as M2's workflow does (the
kernel's `vm.max_map_count`, swap off, transparent huge pages on `madvise`,
JDK 21 and Go 1.24), built the harness, and made one invocation of
`run_all.sh --scale full --only idle-memory` from {{ ver(M3, "date_utc") }}: five
repetitions of every `idle-memory` cell with the Go and Java controls, without
the quiet gate, inside `sudo systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=14G -p
MemorySwapMax=0`. The workflow uploads the results directory as the artifact
`taskbench-cell-41548553538`, after the run's id. Unlike M2's, this scope
is below the machine's memory ({{ gib(spec(M3, "cgroup_memory_max")) }} GiB against
{{ gib(spec(M3, "mem_total_bytes")) }} GiB), as M1's {{ gib(spec(M1, "cgroup_memory_max")) }} GiB
cgroup is, so the scope bounds what the memory guard sees. No cell met the
guard: every cell created all its tasks in all five runs, 1,000,000 at 1M,
except those of the guard-page strategies, which failed at the mapping limit
({{ created(M3, "A/1M", 1000000) }} tasks).

Pre-registration commit: none yet. Thresholds are copied from proposal/20 at
commit `e93cd8a` (draft 1.0, not frozen); there are no amendments beyond A1-A6,
which are in that text.

Every table can be regenerated with
`python3 summarize.py --markdown results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1 results/github-runner/full-38518035636 results/github-runner/cell-41548553538`
(medians [min-max] over runs, ratios of medians, 95 % percentile bootstrap
intervals over runs, and section 2.4's rule on each machine and on each pair
of machines).

## 1. Memory per idle task (threshold: within 4x Go at 100k and 1M)

Bytes per task = (VmRSS + VmPTE after - before) / N from `/proc/self/status`,
read one second after all N tasks have parked (`--settle-ms 1000`). Shallow use
is about 1 KiB of frames; the excursion is a 32 KiB recursion of 4000-byte
frames that touches every page, after which the task parks again. For Go the
after-excursion figure of record is read after four `runtime.GC()` calls and
`debug.FreeOSMemory()` (policy 4); Java's after `System.gc()`. C's exact size is
its nominal size plus the excursion, as a compiler bounding the whole task body
would choose (4 KiB + 32 KiB = 36 KiB, and so on). Ratio = median over Go's
median on the same machine; ranges in brackets are the five runs' minimum and
maximum, and for ratios the conservative [min/max, max/min] that section 2.4
uses. All three machines ran every cell five times at 100k and at 1M tasks (M3
ran this benchmark alone); where M2's memory guard stopped a cell, the table
shows the stop and the range of tasks created over the runs. M3's guard stopped
none.

{{ table("mem") }}

Section 2.4 of proposal/20, applied to each machine and then to pairs of
machines: each stackful configuration's worst ratio w over the four conditions
(at 1M after the excursion, where Go's own cell fits no machine, the larger of
the ratios to Go after GC at 100k and to Go shallow at 1M, a lower bound of Go
after GC at 1M). A configuration passes if w is at or below 4 on both machines,
fails if it is above 4 on both, and is inconclusive otherwise; a cell whose
conservative range crosses 4, or that the memory guard stopped, makes it
inconclusive on that machine. The rule names two machines, M1 and the
reviewer's M2. With a third, it is applied here to M1 with each runner: with
M2, which ran every benchmark, and with M3, which ran this one on a 16 GiB
machine, the size section 4.4 asks for, and holds the million-task cells that
M2 could not. Which pair decides the primary is the owner's to confirm; the
verdict of section 9.1 is the same on either.

{{ table("rule_pairs") }}

The four conditions on each machine (ratio to Go, its 95 % bootstrap interval
over the runs, and the conservative range):

**M1**

{{ table("rule_m1") }}

