**M3**

{{ table("rule_m3") }}

* **The verdict.** D(B) is the only stackful strategy that passes. At 128 KiB it
  passes on all three machines, with w = {{ trio(w(M1, "D(B)/128K"), w(M2, "D(B)/128K"), w(M3, "D(B)/128K")) }}, which is what section 2.4's primary asks of at
  least one stackful strategy (section 9.1). At 1 MiB and 8 MiB it passes on M1
  (w = {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} and {{ w(M1, "D(B)/8M") }}) and on M3
  (w = {{ w(M3, "D(B)/1M") }} and {{ w(M3, "D(B)/8M") }}), so section 2.4 passes it at
  every reservation on M1 and M3. At the 1 MiB default, M3's million-task cells
  hold {{ im(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} KiB per task in the shallow phase and
  {{ im(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion") }} KiB after the excursion (M1:
  {{ im(M1, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} and {{ im(M1, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion") }} KiB):
  {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }}x Go shallow at 1M, and after the excursion
  {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }}x Go after
  GC at 100k and {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion", 1000000, "shallow") }}x Go
  shallow at 1M, the worst of the four conditions (conservative range
  {{ lohi(M3, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=1000000", "total_per_task_shallow") }},
  far from 4). On M2 both 100k
  conditions pass at those reservations ({{ imr(M2, "D(B)/1M", 100000) }}x and
  {{ imr(M2, "D(B)/1M", 100000, "excursion") }}x at 1 MiB,
  {{ imr(M2, "D(B)/8M", 100000) }}x and {{ imr(M2, "D(B)/8M", 100000, "excursion") }}x
  at 8 MiB), but the 1M cells are failed rows, stopped by the memory guard at
  {{ created(M2, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} and {{ created(M2, "D(B)/8M", 1000000) }}
  tasks, so section 2.4 calls these two configurations inconclusive on M2, not
  failed, and inconclusive on M1 and M2 together. Over the tasks they had
  created when the guard stopped them, the D(B)/1 MiB runs held
  {{ med(M2, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB per task,
  as at 100k ({{ im(M2, "D(B)/1M", 100000) }} KiB); that is not a measurement of the
  cell, and no verdict uses it.
* **The others fail on every machine.** B and C keep the excursion's pages
  ("commit creep": after the excursion B/1 MiB holds
  {{ trio(im(M1, "B/1M", 100000, "excursion"), im(M2, "B/1M", 100000, "excursion"), im(M3, "B/1M", 100000, "excursion"), "KiB per task") }})
  and fail
  against Go after its collection ({{ im(M1, "go", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }},
  {{ im(M2, "go", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }} and
  {{ im(M3, "go", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }} KiB), although measured
  against Go before its collection ({{ im(M1, "go", 100000, "excursion") }},
  {{ im(M2, "go", 100000, "excursion") }} and {{ im(M3, "go", 100000, "excursion") }} KiB)
  B/1 MiB is at
  {{ rat(M1, IM, "B/1M N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion") }}x,
  {{ rat(M2, IM, "B/1M N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion") }}x
  and {{ rat(M3, IM, "B/1M N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion") }}x.
  Their million-task cells, the shallow phase only (policy 7), are within 4x
  wherever they were measured (on M3: B {{ imr(M3, "B/128K", 1000000) }}x,
  {{ imr(M3, "B/1M", 1000000) }}x and {{ imr(M3, "B/8M", 1000000) }}x at 128 KiB, 1 MiB
  and 8 MiB, C {{ imr(M3, "C/36K", 1000000) }}x to {{ imr(M3, "C/48K", 1000000) }}x), so
  the excursion at 100k decides them, on every machine.
  A and D(A) fail on scale first: each guard page splits a mapping, and the
  process stops at {{ med(M1, IM, "A/1M N=100000", "tasks_created", "", 0) }} tasks on
  M1, {{ med(M2, IM, "A/1M N=100000", "tasks_created", "", 0) }} on M2 and
  {{ created(M3, "A/1M", 100000) }} on M3 under
  `vm.max_map_count` 65530 (their per-task figures over the tasks that were
  created match B's and D(B)'s).
* **The machines agree.** Over every B, C and D(B) configuration at 100k, the
  three machines' ratios to Go differ by at most
  {{ mem_spread(["B/128K", "B/1M", "B/8M", "C/36K", "C/40K", "C/48K", "D(B)/128K", "D(B)/1M", "D(B)/8M"], machines=(M1, M2, M3)) }}
  (D(B) alone: at most {{ mem_spread(["D(B)/128K", "D(B)/1M", "D(B)/8M"], machines=(M1, M2, M3)) }}),
  and at 1M, where M1 and M3 measured every one of them, their shallow ratios
  differ by at most
  {{ mem_spread(["B/128K", "B/1M", "B/8M", "C/36K", "C/40K", "C/48K", "D(B)/128K", "D(B)/1M", "D(B)/8M"], 1000000, (M1, M3), ("shallow",)) }}.
  D(B)/128 KiB's worst ratio is {{ trio(w(M1, "D(B)/128K"), w(M2, "D(B)/128K"), w(M3, "D(B)/128K")) }}, and D(B)/1 MiB's
  {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} on M1 and {{ w(M3, "D(B)/1M") }} on M3. The most variable cell is a
  control's: Go's after-excursion figure before its GC, whose five runs span
  {{ vmin(M2, IM, "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "KiB") }}-{{ vmax(M2, IM, "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "KiB") }}
  KiB on M2 and
  {{ vmin(M3, IM, "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "KiB") }}-{{ vmax(M3, IM, "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "KiB") }}
  KiB on M3; it is not a baseline of record.
* **What a task costs.** D(B)/1 MiB holds
  {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB resident per
  task at 1M on M1 (one stack page plus the task record) and
  {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB of page
  tables, and M3 {{ rng(M3, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }}
  and {{ rng(M3, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB at 1M;
  M2 measured {{ rng(M2, IM, "D(B)/1M N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }}
  and {{ rng(M2, IM, "D(B)/1M N=100000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB at 100k.
  A 4 KiB page-table page maps 2 MiB, which two 1 MiB slots share. The
  reservation costs page tables, not resident memory: at 1M on M1, 128 KiB slots
  need {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/128K N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB and
  8 MiB slots {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/8M N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB
  (M2: {{ rng(M2, IM, "D(B)/128K N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB at
  128 KiB and 1M, {{ rng(M2, IM, "D(B)/8M N=100000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }}
  KiB at 8 MiB and 100k). Measured on RSS alone, as section 6.5 literally
  defines the resident set, the stackful strategies' ratios are smaller still
  (D(B)/1 MiB at 100k, shallow:
  {{ trio(rat(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "go N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow") + "x", rat(M2, IM, "D(B)/1M N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "go N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow") + "x", rat(M3, IM, "D(B)/1M N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "go N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow") + "x") }};
  the RSS-only columns of `summarize.py`), because Go's page tables are
  negligible and theirs are not.
* **Decommit on park.** D(B) made
  {{ num(notefield(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "tasks_created", "decommits")) }}
  `madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)` calls for 1M tasks on M1,
  {{ num(notefield(M2, IM, "D(B)/128K N=1000000", "tasks_created", "decommits")) }} in
  M2's D(B)/128 KiB cell at 1M, and
  {{ num(notefield(M3, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "tasks_created", "decommits")) }} in
  M3's D(B)/1 MiB cell at 1M (the `decommits=` field of the rows' `note`): one
  per task, at the park after its excursion. The park after shallow use made
  none, because the probe watermark showed that the stack had not grown below
  its top page.
* **1M tasks.** Per-task memory does not change from 100k to 1M for any
  stackful strategy on M1 or M3, nor on M2 for the cells it completed. At 1M, A, B, C
  and Go measured the shallow phase only (policy 7): keeping the excursion's
  pages for 1M tasks needs about
  {{ f"{cell(M1, IM, 'B/1M N=100000').med('total_per_task_excursion') * 1e6 / 2**30:.0f}" }} GiB
  (B/1 MiB's figure at 100k times 1M).
* **C and the 5 KiB reserve.** C's shallow figure is
  {{ trio(med(M1, IM, "C/36K N=100000", "total_per_task_shallow", "KiB"), med(M2, IM, "C/36K N=100000", "total_per_task_shallow", "KiB"), med(M3, IM, "C/36K N=100000", "total_per_task_shallow", "KiB"), "KiB") }}, against {{ smoke_im("C/36K", 10000) }} KiB in M2's smoke run of harness 0.1.0 (10k
  tasks, one run), whose reserve was 8 KiB. A slot of 36 + 5 KiB is not a whole
  number of pages, so a quarter of the slots have their top frames straddle a
  page boundary and touch two pages. Rounding C's slots to pages (the reserve
  absorbing the rounding) would restore the earlier figure; C does not pass
  either way.
* **The controls.** Java's virtual threads copy a parked thread's frames into
  heap chunks, so Java's figures ({{ im(M1, "java", 1000000) }} KiB shallow at 1M on
  M1, {{ im(M2, "java", 1000000) }} KiB on M2, {{ im(M3, "java", 1000000) }} KiB on M3)
  are a managed baseline, not a non-moving-stack precedent. E and tokio keep no
  stack per task (E {{ trio(im(M1, "E", 1000000), im(M2, "E", 1000000), im(M3, "E", 1000000), "KiB") }},
  tokio {{ trio(im(M1, "tokio", 1000000), im(M2, "tokio", 1000000), im(M3, "tokio", 1000000), "KiB") }}). Go's
  before-GC figure after the excursion, {{ im(M2, "go", 100000, "excursion") }} KiB
  on M2 with transparent huge pages on `madvise`, was
  {{ smoke_im("go", 10000, "excursion") }} KiB in M2's smoke run under the image's
  `always` (10k tasks, one run), which is why the workflow sets `madvise`; after
  GC it was {{ smoke_im("go", 10000, "excursion_after_gc") }} KiB there and is
  {{ im(M2, "go", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }} KiB here.

## 2. Spawn plus join (threshold: median within 3x Go with pooled stacks)

100k spawn-plus-join pairs after 10k unrecorded pairs (Java: 20k, for its JIT),
each timed with a monotonic clock; the trivial task returns at once.

{{ table("spawn_join") }}

* **Verdict.** B's median is {{ rat(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }} times
  Go's on M1 ({{ ci(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}) and
  {{ rat(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }} times on M2
  ({{ ci(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}): within 3x on both machines
  (**holds**). The machines differ by
  {{ spread(rat(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go"), rat(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go")) }}:
  B costs {{ rng(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M1 and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M2, Go
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "go", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "go", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us. D(B), the candidate, costs
  about what B costs on each machine
  ({{ rat(M1, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x and
  {{ rat(M2, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x Go): a task that never grew
  below its top page is released without a system call.
* **The cost of mapping.** A maps, protects and unmaps a stack per task
  ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "A/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M1,
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "A/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M2); with a cache of 1024
  freed stacks it costs {{ rng(M1, SJ, "A/1M stack_cache=1024", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }}
  and {{ rng(M2, SJ, "A/1M stack_cache=1024", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us. D(A)
  with the cache still pays one `madvise` per release
  ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "D(A)/1M stack_cache=1024", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "D(A)/1M stack_cache=1024", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us), which
  D(B)'s watermark avoids.
* **Tails.** B's p99 is below Go's on both machines
  ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p99", "us") }} against
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "go", "spawn_join_p99", "us") }} us on M1,
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p99", "us") }} against
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "go", "spawn_join_p99", "us") }} us on M2).
* **The load during M2's pooled cells.** During each of M2's B, C and D(B)
  processes ({{ walls(M2, SJ, SJ_POOLED) }} of wall-clock time) `load.csv` counts
  other activity of {{ others(M2, SJ, SJ_POOLED) }} CPUs, against
  {{ others(M2, SJ, SJ_UNPOOLED) }} during A's, E's, tokio's and Go's, and
  {{ others(M1, SJ, SJ_POOLED) }} during the same cells' processes on M1. B's
  fastest run on M2 ({{ least_loaded(M2, SJ, SJ_POOLED[0], "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us")[1] }} us)
  is the one with the least of it
  ({{ least_loaded(M2, SJ, SJ_POOLED[0], "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us")[0] }} CPUs). The run
  does not record what that activity was (on a kernel that accounts interrupt
  time separately, `load.sh` counts a process's own interrupt and softirq time
  as other processes', section 10.3), so whether it slowed these cells, and
  explains part of the spread between the machines, is not known; it does not
  bring B near the bar.
* **The cells that differ most between the machines** are controls and
  variants, not verdict inputs: Java's virtual threads cost
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "java", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us per spawn and join on M1 and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "java", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M2, both after 20,000
  unrecorded rounds for the JIT ({{ rat(M1, SJ, "java", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x
  and {{ rat(M2, SJ, "java", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x Go), and B with batches of
  100 costs {{ rng(M1, SJ, "B/1M batch=100", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "B/1M batch=100", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us per task.

## 3. Scale: 1M idle tasks on a 16 GiB machine with default sysctls

`max-tasks --tasks 1000000 --mem-guard 512M --hold-secs 60`: tasks that do
shallow work and park are spawned one at a time until the cap, a failed spawn,
or less than 512 MiB of memory headroom (`MemAvailable`, or the memory cgroup's
headroom when smaller); a run that reaches the cap then keeps every task parked
for 60 seconds, checks that all of them are still parked, and reads memory
again (section 6.5: "pass if all park and the process survives 60 seconds").
OS threads have a cap of 30,000.

{{ table("max_tasks") }}

* A and D(A) stop at {{ med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks on
  M1 and {{ med(M2, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} on M2, the process then
  holding {{ pair(med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "vmas_at_stop", "", 0), med(M2, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "vmas_at_stop", "", 0), "mappings") }}:
  every task's guard page splits its stack mapping, so the process meets `vm.max_map_count`
  (65530) at about 32k tasks. That is the scale row's fail condition, met on both
  machines: **guard-page-per-task is out** as a default, which policy 3 already
  concludes.
* B, C, D(B), E and tokio, and the Go and Java controls, park 1M tasks on both
  machines; after the 60-second hold every task is still parked, and per-task
  memory is unchanged (B/1 MiB
  {{ rng(M1, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_at_stop", "KiB") }} KiB at the stop
  and {{ rng(M1, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_after_hold", "KiB") }} KiB after
  the hold on M1, {{ rng(M2, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_at_stop", "KiB") }}
  and {{ rng(M2, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_after_hold", "KiB") }} KiB on
  M2). The pooled strategies' reservations do not approach the mapping limit:
  B/1 MiB has {{ med(M1, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "vmas_at_stop", "", 0) }} mappings in
  all at 1M tasks on M1 and {{ med(M2, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "vmas_at_stop", "", 0) }}
  on M2, with about
  {{ f"{int(notefield(M1, IM, 'B/1M N=1000000', 'tasks_created', 'reserved_bytes')) / (1 << 40):.2f}" }}
  TiB of address space reserved in 1 GiB chunks (`reserved_bytes` in the
  `idle-memory` rows' note on M1).
* OS threads stop at {{ med(M1, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} on M1
  ({{ ", ".join(sorted({n for st, n in cell(M1, MT, "os-threads N=30000").status["limit"]})) }}):
  each Rust thread maps its stack, its guard, and the signal stack that `std`
  maps with its own guard. On M2 they stop at
  {{ med(M2, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }}
  ({{ ", ".join(sorted({n for st, n in cell(M2, MT, "os-threads N=30000").status["limit"]})) }}),
  at the scope's pids cgroup of {{ spec(M2, "cgroup_pids_max") }} tasks.
* M2 has {{ gib(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes"), 2) }} GiB, not the row's 16 GiB. 1M pooled
  tasks fit in it (B/1 MiB: about
  {{ f"{cell(M2, MT, 'B/1M N=1000000').med('total_per_task_at_stop') * 1e6 / 2**30:.1f}" }}
  GiB, beside the 512 MiB margin), and the fail condition is a mapping limit,
  not memory, so the row's verdict does not depend on it.

## 4. Echo (threshold: 10k-connection p99 within 2x Go) and idle connections

`echo`: N closed-loop client tasks in the same process (64-byte messages, a
write and a read of the echo per round trip), 5 s unrecorded then 30 s
recorded; the server is one task per connection. `idle-connections`: N server
tasks parked in `read`, the client ends held as plain sockets, memory read after
30 s idle (section 6.5). Both ends count against `RLIMIT_NOFILE`, so N
connections need 2N + 64 descriptors.

{{ table("echo") }}

* **10k connections ran on M2 only.** On M1 they need 20,064 descriptors and
  the hard limit is {{ num(spec(M1, "rlimit_nofile").split("/")[1]) }} (policy 3: the cell
  runs only where the default limit allows); the harness and the controls
  record failed rows instead. Inside M2's scope the hard limit was
  {{ num(spec(M2, "rlimit_nofile").split("/")[1]) }} (section 10.2), and there every
  stackful strategy's p99 is within 2x Go's: B
  {{ rat(M2, EC, "B/1M connections=10000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}x
  ({{ ci(M2, EC, "B/1M connections=10000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}),
  D(B) {{ rat(M2, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=10000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}x
  ({{ ci(M2, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=10000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}),
  and at most
  {{ maxrat(M2, EC, [x + " connections=10000" for x in STACKFUL_1M], "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}x
  (D(A)); Java's virtual threads reach
  {{ rat(M2, EC, "java connections=10000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}x
  and tokio {{ rat(M2, EC, "tokio connections=10000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=10000") }}x.
  One machine cannot decide the row: it is **not shown** (section 9.1).
* **At 1000 connections** B's p99 is
  {{ rat(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }} times
  Go's on M1 ({{ ci(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }})
  and {{ rat(M2, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }} on
  M2 ({{ ci(M2, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}),
  and D(B)'s {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}
  and {{ rat(M2, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}.
  D(A) is the cell that differs most between the machines:
  {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x Go on
  M1 ({{ ci(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }})
  and {{ rat(M2, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x on
  M2 ({{ ci(M2, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}),
  a spread of
  {{ spread(rat(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000"), rat(M2, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000")) }}.
  Without a watermark it makes a `madvise` call at every park, and every
  request parks its server task and its client task; that costs D(A)
  {{ f"{(cell(M1, EC, 'D(A)/1M connections=1000').med('echo_rtt_p50') - cell(M1, EC, 'B/1M connections=1000').med('echo_rtt_p50')) / 1e6:.1f}" }}
  ms of median round trip over B on M1 and
  {{ f"{(cell(M2, EC, 'D(A)/1M connections=1000').med('echo_rtt_p50') - cell(M2, EC, 'B/1M connections=1000').med('echo_rtt_p50')) / 1e6:.1f}" }}
  ms on M2. tokio's p99 also differs
  ({{ rat(M1, EC, "tokio connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x and
  {{ rat(M2, EC, "tokio connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x
  Go). I/O design, for the record: taskbench's workers poll one shared epoll
  instance (busy workers every 31 scheduling decisions, idle ones before they
  spin) and one sleeping worker blocks in it; Go uses its integrated netpoller.
  A miss would be "runtime design review, not a language change".
* **Idle connections.** M1 ran them at 9,900 connections, the most its
  descriptor limit allows, and M2 at the registered 100,000. A pooled
  strategy's idle connection costs
  {{ pair(rng(M1, IC, "B/1M connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "KiB"), rng(M2, IC, "B/1M connections=100000", "total_per_connection", "KiB"), "KiB") }}
  (B/1 MiB; for C/8 KiB, whose small slots need fewer page tables,
  {{ rng(M1, IC, "C/8K connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, IC, "C/8K connections=100000", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }} KiB),
  against Go's {{ rng(M1, IC, "go connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }}
  and {{ rng(M2, IC, "go connections=100000", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }} KiB, so
  the ratio is {{ rat(M1, IC, "B/1M connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "go connections=9900") }}x
  on M1 and {{ rat(M2, IC, "B/1M connections=100000", "total_per_connection", "go connections=100000") }}x
  on M2: Go's per-connection cost is lower at M2's 100,000 connections than at
  M1's 9,900, and the pooled strategies' is the same at both, so the two ratios
  are not one cell measured twice. The server task holds a 4 KiB read buffer on
  its stack.
  A and D(A) failed at 100k on M2 in every run with "{{ note(M2, IC, "A/1M connections=100000", "idle_connections") }}".
  A server task per connection needs a guarded mapping each, so the acceptor's
  `spawn` meets the VMA limit near
  {{ med(M2, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks, the acceptor stops,
  and the client side's blocking `connect` times out on the full listen
  backlog. The CSV records only the timeout; the rest is the harness's code
  path (`src/bench/echo.rs`), not a recorded field.

## 5. Stack exhaustion (threshold: no segfault, ever)

`deep-recursion --framed` in every stackful configuration (E and tokio run the
parse and the naive recursion on the worker's 8 MiB stack, plus a recursion of
boxed futures): a 10k-deep recursive-descent parse (four frames per level), a
1M-deep naive recursion with a drop guard every 256 frames, and the framed
recursion (a 256-byte local and a drop guard in every frame), each in its own
task, then a task that checks the runtime still works. The reserve columns are
the bytes of the 5 KiB probe reserve the unwind overwrote (the reserve is
painted before the case and scanned after), the maximum over five runs.

{{ table("deep") }}

* **Every exhaustion is clean** on both machines: {{ outcomes(M1) }} outcome rows
  on M1 and {{ outcomes(M2) }} on M2, {{ outcomes(M1, False) + outcomes(M2, False) }}
  unclean, and the follow-up task ran in every run. `process_exit` rows
  (crashes) across every CSV of the three results directories: **{{ crashes() }}**.
* **Mechanism.** B, C and D(B) fail through the software probe and unwind, so
  every destructor runs (0 drop guards skipped). A and D(A) fail through the
  guard page; the trap handler ends the task without unwinding, so the frames'
  destructors are skipped (in the naive recursion at 1 MiB,
  {{ pair(med(M1, DR, "A/1M", "naive_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0), med(M2, DR, "A/1M", "naive_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0), "drop guards") }};
  in the framed one,
  {{ pair(med(M1, DR, "A/1M", "framed_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0), med(M2, DR, "A/1M", "framed_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0)) }}).
* **The probe reserve.** The largest unwind used
  {{ pair(f"{reserve_max(M1):,}", f"{reserve_max(M2):,}", "bytes of the 5,120-byte reserve") }}. Before
  the fix of section 10.3, the first unwind of each process used about 5 KiB;
  M2's smoke run of harness 0.1.0, made before the fix, shows that pattern
  ({{ smoke_vmax(DR, "B/1M", "parse_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} bytes for the
  first unwind, {{ smoke_vmax(DR, "B/1M", "naive_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} for
  the next), and M2's full run of 0.2.0 does not.
* **Depth per reservation.** The 10k-deep parse completes only at 8 MiB (and on
  E and tokio, whose worker stacks are 8 MiB); at 1 MiB it fails cleanly at
  level {{ pair(med(M1, DR, "B/1M", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0), med(M2, DR, "B/1M", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0)) }}. Wherever the table
  gives two depths, the machines' medians differ by
  {{ depth_spread() }} level at most. B's usable stack is a little over its reservation (the slot is
  rounded up to a page above the 5 KiB reserve), so B reaches a few more levels
  than A.
* The corpus-wide `--stats stack-depth` histogram (interpreter era) is reported
  separately and decides whether the default reservation suffices.

## 6. Starvation (threshold: probe p99 no worse than Go and under 10 ms)

Four compute tasks per worker (16 on M1's four workers, 8 on M2's two) run an
LCG loop and reach a yield point every K iterations, the stand-in for a
compiler-inserted yield check; a probe task sleeps 1 ms in a loop and records
how late each wake-up is, first alone (1 s) and then under the load (11 s). Go
runs the same loop with no yields at all and relies on its default
asynchronous preemption (policy 8); Java has no preemption of virtual threads
and runs with no yields and with `Thread.yield()` every K iterations. Labels:
the default K is 10000; `yield_every=0` means no yield points. In an "M1 / M2"
column, a single figure means that both machines measured the same.

{{ table("starvation") }}

* **With yield points the probe's p99 is far below Go's on both machines**: on
  M1 {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us at K = 10k (slices
  of {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) and
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us at
  K = 100k, against {{ rng(M1, ST, "go yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms
  for Go's preemption; on M2
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us (slices of
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) and
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us,
  against {{ rng(M2, ST, "go yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms. The
  threshold holds for every strategy on both machines.
* **Without yield points** (`yield_every=0`) the probe cannot run until the
  compute tasks stop: {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }}
  ms on M1 and {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }}
  ms on M2. Policy 8 expects this and reports it without counting it.
* **What yield points cost.** Compute throughput with no yield points is
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }}
  iterations per second on M1 and
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }} on M2.
  Relative to that median, a yield point every 100k iterations changes the
  median throughput by {{ chg(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  on M1 and {{ chg(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  on M2, every 10k by {{ chg(M1, ST, "B/1M", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  and {{ chg(M2, ST, "B/1M", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %,
  and every 1k by {{ chg(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  and {{ chg(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %.
  On M1 the five-run ranges (the one above, and
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }} at 10k) are wider
  than the first two differences, so at 10k and 100k iterations the cost is
  below what M1 resolves. M2, where the other processes used at most
  {{ load_max(M2, ST) }} CPUs during any starvation process, resolves them: its ranges
  at 10k ({{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }}) and at 100k
  ({{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }})
  lie below the whole range without yield points. At 1k (slices of
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) the
  cost is clear on both. This loop yields between chunks of K iterations and
  checks nothing in between, so these are the costs of the switches alone; the
  per-iteration check a compiler would insert is the `loop` workload of
  section 7, which costs nothing measurable on M1 and
  {{ rng(M2, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % on M2. Because
  policy 8 puts yield points in the 3 % budget, the yield interval and the
  check's placement are design parameters: slices of about 0.2 ms (K = 100k)
  keep the probe's p99 at
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us on M1
  and {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us on
  M2, {{ f"{cell(M1, ST, 'go yield_every=0').med('probe_loaded_late_p99') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=100000').med('probe_loaded_late_p99'):.0f}" }}
  and {{ f"{cell(M2, ST, 'go yield_every=0').med('probe_loaded_late_p99') / cell(M2, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=100000').med('probe_loaded_late_p99'):.0f}" }}
  times lower than Go's.
* tokio's p99 of about 1 ms on both machines
  ({{ rng(M1, ST, "tokio", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "tokio", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us) is its timer's
  granularity, not starvation. OS threads rely on the kernel's preemption.

## 7. Software probe overhead (threshold: at most 3 % on compute loops)

Each workload is compiled without probes and with three probe models, and the
four variants run alternately 31 times inside one task on the strategy's stack;
a cell's value is the ratio of medians minus one, and the table shows the
median and range over five processes on each machine. `split` is the model of
record: `cmp rsp, fs:[0x70]; jb`, the prologue check of GCC's `-fsplit-stack`,
against a slot the scheduler keeps equal to the running task's limit. `arg`
passes the limit as an argument; `tls` reads a Rust thread-local (not
migration-safe). `loop` is the call-free LCG loop of section 6 with the same
check on every back-edge: the cost model of a compiler-inserted yield check,
which policy 8 counts in this budget (in the other workloads the entry check can
double as the yield point, as Go's prologue check did before asynchronous
preemption).

{{ table("probe") }}

* **Taken at face value, two of the four workloads exceed 3 % on each machine,
  but not the same two.** On M1, `fib` does in every run on every strategy (B:
  {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %; the lowest single
  run of any strategy,
  {{ f"{min(min(cell(M1, PO, l).values['fib_split_probe_overhead']) for l in ['A/1M', 'B/1M', 'C/8K', 'D(B)/1M', 'E']):.1f}" }} %),
  and `tree`'s median does on every strategy (B:
  {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %), although single
  runs fall below it; `calls`
  ({{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "calls_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %) and the back-edge
  yield check ({{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %) are
  within it, the check because its compare and branch run in parallel with the
  loop's multiply chain. On M2, `fib` exceeds it as well (B:
  {{ rng(M2, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %; lowest run of any
  strategy
  {{ f"{min(min(cell(M2, PO, l).values['fib_split_probe_overhead']) for l in ['A/1M', 'B/1M', 'C/8K', 'D(B)/1M', 'E']):.1f}" }} %),
  and so does the back-edge check, in every run of every strategy (B:
  {{ rng(M2, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %; lowest run
  {{ f"{min(min(cell(M2, PO, l).values['loop_split_probe_overhead']) for l in ['A/1M', 'B/1M', 'C/8K', 'D(B)/1M', 'E']):.1f}" }} %),
  while `tree` ({{ rng(M2, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %) and
  `calls` ({{ rng(M2, PO, "B/1M", "calls_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %) are
  within it. For B the machines differ by
  {{ spread(med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1), med(M2, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1), 1) }}
  points on `fib`,
  {{ spread(med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1), med(M2, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1), 1) }}
  on `tree` and
  {{ spread(med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1), med(M2, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1), 1) }}
  on the back-edge check.
* **Why this does not decide the bar.** `fib` is an upper bound by
  construction: LLVM turns one of its two recursive calls into a loop that keeps
  the probe written at the top of the source function, so the check runs about
  twice per machine-level call, which a compiler inserting it into the prologue
  after optimisation would not do. `calls`' callee is a small leaf that a
  compiler would exempt (Go's `NOSPLIT` rule); `tree` is a fair case. There are
  no hardware counters on either machine ("{{ note(M1, PO, "B/1M", "instruction_counts") }}"
  on M1, "{{ note(M2, PO, "B/1M", "instruction_counts") }}" on M2), so only wall-clock
  medians exist: between the five processes of B's cell the without-probes time
  of `fib` moved by
  {{ f"{(max(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['fib_without_probes']) / min(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['fib_without_probes']) - 1) * 100:.2f}" }} %
  on M1 and
  {{ f"{(max(cell(M2, PO, 'B/1M').values['fib_without_probes']) / min(cell(M2, PO, 'B/1M').values['fib_without_probes']) - 1) * 100:.2f}" }} %
  on M2, and that of `tree` by
  {{ f"{(max(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['tree_without_probes']) / min(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['tree_without_probes']) - 1) * 100:.2f}" }} %
  and
  {{ f"{(max(cell(M2, PO, 'B/1M').values['tree_without_probes']) / min(cell(M2, PO, 'B/1M').values['tree_without_probes']) - 1) * 100:.2f}" }} %.
  M2's runs are steadier than M1's, but the two machines' figures for `fib` and
  for the back-edge check differ by more than the bar itself, so the overhead
  depends on the processor as much as on the probe. The owner's policy 1 therefore reports the bar as measured and leaves
  the decision to the native era, on a machine with a PMU and a fixed
  frequency, with Sisu's own probe placement. The measurements are a warning
  for that decision, not a pass, and M2 adds one: a check on every back-edge
  can by itself cost more than the whole budget.
* **Not measured:** a vectorisable loop. A check inserted before vectorisation
  would prevent it, so a compiler must place yield checks after loop
  optimisation (per vector iteration, or every N iterations).

## 8. io_uring cancellation into stack buffers (amendment A1, D-058)

Two procedures per stackful strategy, five runs each on each machine, 1M reads
per run in batches of 8,192 in flight. `pipe_mix=cancel` is A1 as registered:
every read targets an empty pipe, so it stays in flight, and every one is
cancelled through its task; the cancelled call submits
`IORING_OP_ASYNC_CANCEL` and returns only on the read's own completion;
afterwards late data is written into every pipe, so any read the kernel still
held would land, and every buffer (4 KiB, on the task's stack, the frame kept
alive until the batch ends) is checked again. The default race mix (512-byte
buffers) writes data for a quarter of the reads, cancels a quarter, and does
both, in either order, for half. B also runs the file-backed variants:
`O_DIRECT` reads cancelled while the device holds them (`-ENOENT`, and a later
completion), and buffered reads with `IOSQE_ASYNC` (`-EALREADY`).
`--break-contract` is the negative control: a cancelled task stops waiting at
once. In an "M1 / M2" column, a single figure means that both machines
measured the same.

{{ table("uring") }}

* **Zero, as required, on both machines.** No byte of any buffer changed after
  its call returned, in any run of either procedure on A, B, C, D(A) and D(B),
  nor in the file variants: {{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M + ["B/1M source=file", "B/1M source=file-buffered"], "buffer_writes_after_return") }}
  bytes over {{ runs_of(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M + ["B/1M source=file", "B/1M source=file-buffered"], "buffer_writes_after_return") }}
  runs on M1 and {{ sumcells(M2, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M + ["B/1M source=file", "B/1M source=file-buffered"], "buffer_writes_after_return") }}
  bytes over {{ runs_of(M2, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M + ["B/1M source=file", "B/1M source=file-buffered"], "buffer_writes_after_return") }}
  on M2. The negative control writes
  {{ total(M1, CU, "B/1M break_contract=true", "buffer_writes_after_return") }} bytes
  over its five runs on M1 and
  {{ total(M2, CU, "B/1M break_contract=true", "buffer_writes_after_return") }} on M2,
  so the check sees what it is meant to see.
* **A cancel that cannot stop the read.** A quarter of the reads carry
  `IOSQE_ASYNC`, which sends them to an io-wq worker thread, where a blocked
  pipe read can only be interrupted. A cancel that finds such a read already
  executing answers `-EALREADY`
  ({{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "cancel_ealready") }}
  times in the {{ runs_of(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "cancel_ealready") }}
  pipe runs above on M1,
  {{ sumcells(M2, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "cancel_ealready") }}
  in as many on M2), and the read usually ends at once. In M1's first
  full-scale group, {{ superseded_stuck()[0] }} of
  {{ f"{superseded_stuck()[4]:,}" }} pipe reads did not: each stayed blocked
  until the harness wrote its late data at the round's 30-second deadline, and
  each call returned only after that completion,
  {{ f"{superseded_stuck()[1] / 1e3:.3f}" }}-{{ f"{superseded_stuck()[2] / 1e3:.3f}" }} s after
  its cancel (the slowest cancellation of each of the {{ superseded_stuck()[3] }} runs
  concerned), with its buffer intact afterwards. In the runs above,
  {{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "completed_with_late_data") }}
  reads did on M1 and
  {{ sumcells(M2, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "completed_with_late_data") }}
  on M2 (`completed_with_late_data`). Waiting is what keeps a lent buffer safe,
  and D-058 requires it; the consequence is that a cancelled task waits as long
  as an uncancellable read does. That is a liveness question for the native
  runtime (re-issuing the cancel signals the worker again; pollable files can
  use poll-armed reads, which a cancel always stops), not a memory-safety one,
  and it reopens nothing. M1's first group's check counted these reads' late
  data as "written at return"; section 10.3 describes the fix and where the
  superseded rows are kept.
* **The case D-058 exists for** is the file-backed one: a cancel that finds the
  read already issued to the device (`-ENOENT`) while the read still completes
  later ({{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M source=file", "completed_after_failed_cancel", "", 0) }}
  reads per run on M1,
  {{ rng(M2, CU, "B/1M source=file", "completed_after_failed_cancel", "", 0) }} on
  M2); the call waited for those completions, which on M2's disk take much
  longer: the cancel-to-return median of that variant is
  {{ rng(M2, CU, "B/1M source=file", "cancel_to_return_p50", "ms") }} ms on M2,
  against {{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M source=file", "cancel_to_return_p50", "ms") }} ms on M1.
* **Cancel-to-return latency** in the registered procedure is
  {{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M pipe_mix=cancel", "cancel_to_return_p50", "ms") }} ms median and
  {{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M pipe_mix=cancel", "cancel_to_return_p99", "ms") }} ms p99 for B on
  M1, and {{ rng(M2, CU, "B/1M pipe_mix=cancel", "cancel_to_return_p50", "ms") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, CU, "B/1M pipe_mix=cancel", "cancel_to_return_p99", "ms") }} ms on M2: the
  harness cancels 8,192 reads in a burst, and each cancellation queues one
  request to the ring's thread, so the figure is dominated by the burst, not by
  one cancellation. M1's group ran before the quiet gate existed (section
  10.2), so its latencies may include other work's CPU use; none of these
  latencies is a verdict input.

## 9. Verdict and Q-6 recommendation

### 9.1 Verdict per bet-3 row (harness era; provisional, see the status note)

Section 2.1 of proposal/20: **holds** means the threshold is met with the
pre-specified effect; **fails** means it is missed or a guard trips; anything
else, including too little data or machines that disagree, is **not shown**,
and no fallback is built on it. Section 2.4 states the two-machine rule for the
primary, and the plan asks for every result on two machines, so every row below
holds or fails only if both machines say so. M3 ran the primary's benchmark
alone; the rule is applied to M1 with M2 and to M1 with M3 (section 1), and the
primary holds on either pair. Section 7.8: only the primary (memory) decides
the bet, the guard (exhaustion in ordinary code) can only stop it, and each
secondary triggers its own action.

