**Bet 3 at week 8: holds, provisionally.** Through its primary: D(B), the
default strategy, passes section 2.4 at 128 KiB on all three machines (w =
{{ trio(w(M1, "D(B)/128K"), w(M2, "D(B)/128K"), w(M3, "D(B)/128K")) }}), and at every
reservation on M1 and M3, the two machines with 16 GiB. At the 1 MiB default,
D(B) holds within the pre-registered bound of 4x Go at a million tasks on the
16 GiB runner: {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }}x Go shallow, and after the
excursion {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }}x Go
after its GC at 100k and {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion", 1000000, "shallow") }}x
Go shallow at 1M, so w = {{ w(M3, "D(B)/1M") }} (conservative range
{{ lohi(M3, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=1000000", "total_per_task_shallow") }})
against {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} on M1, and w = {{ w(M3, "D(B)/8M") }} at 8 MiB. B and C fail
on every machine: their million-task cells, which policy 7 requires in the
shallow phase only, are within the bound on M3 (B {{ imr(M3, "B/1M", 1000000) }}x at
1 MiB and {{ imr(M3, "B/8M", 1000000) }}x at 8 MiB, C {{ imr(M3, "C/36K", 1000000) }}x to
{{ imr(M3, "C/48K", 1000000) }}x), but after the excursion at 100k they exceed it
there as on M1 and M2 (B/1 MiB {{ imr(M3, "B/1M", 100000, "excursion") }}x and C
{{ imr(M3, "C/36K", 100000, "excursion") }}x Go after its GC). The machines agree wherever they
measured, and only M2 could not hold D(B)'s 1M cells at 1 MiB and 8 MiB. No
guard has tripped: the exhaustion guard is decided at week 40, against the
default reservation chosen here, and can still stop the bet. The verdict, like
every verdict in this report, stays provisional until the pre-registration is
frozen.

### 9.2 Q-6 recommendation (provisional, harness era)

* **Strategy: D(B)**, pooled stacks carved from large reservations, with
  software probes and decommit on park, the probe doubling as the decommit
  watermark. It is the only stackful strategy that passes the memory primary:
  on all three machines at 128 KiB, on M1 and M3 at every reservation, and on
  M2 in every condition it could measure. B and C fail it by commit creep (an
  excursion's pages stay resident) on every machine, and A and D(A) cannot
  create 100k tasks under default sysctls on any. Its cost over B is small: per spawn
  plus join {{ rng(M1, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} against
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M1 and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} against
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us on M2; echo p99 at 1000
  connections {{ rng(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "ms") }}
  against {{ rng(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "ms") }} ms on M1
  and {{ rng(M2, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "ms") }} against
  {{ rng(M2, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "ms") }} ms on M2. Decommit
  is needed, and its system call shows only where it is paid on every park:
  D(A) has no watermark and makes one `madvise` per park, and its echo
  throughput is {{ rng(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_throughput", "", 0) }}
  requests per second against D(B)'s
  {{ rng(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_throughput", "", 0) }} on M1, and
  {{ rng(M2, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_throughput", "", 0) }} against
  {{ rng(M2, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_throughput", "", 0) }} on M2. In
  `idle-memory`, D(B) made one call per task, at the park after its excursion,
  and none at the park after shallow use.
* **Default reservation: 1 MiB.** Under D(B) a reservation costs page tables,
  not resident memory: on M1, w = {{ w(M1, "D(B)/128K") }},
  {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} and {{ w(M1, "D(B)/8M") }} at 128 KiB, 1 MiB and 8 MiB; on M3,
  w = {{ w(M3, "D(B)/128K") }}, {{ w(M3, "D(B)/1M") }} and {{ w(M3, "D(B)/8M") }}; on M2,
  w = {{ w(M2, "D(B)/128K") }} at 128 KiB, and the worst 100k conditions are
  {{ w100(M2, "D(B)/1M") }} at 1 MiB and {{ w100(M2, "D(B)/8M") }} at 8 MiB. 1 MiB keeps
  a margin under the bar for the runtime's growth, is the size of the JVM's
  default thread stack, and runs
  {{ med(M1, DR, "D(B)/1M", "naive_depth_reached", "", 0) }} levels of a small recursion
  and {{ med(M1, DR, "D(B)/1M", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0) }} levels of the
  four-frame parser before a clean failure, on both machines that ran the
  recursion (M1 and M2). Its 1M cells, which M2
  ({{ gib(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes"), 2) }} GiB) could not hold beside its 1 GiB guard,
  were measured on M3: there D(B)/1 MiB holds
  {{ im(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} KiB per task at 1M tasks, shallow and after the
  excursion, {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }}x Go shallow and
  {{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }}x Go after
  GC ({{ imr(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000, "excursion", 1000000, "shallow") }}x against Go
  shallow at 1M, the stricter substitute), as on M1. Section 2.4 therefore
  passes 1 MiB on M1 and M3, the two machines of the size section 4.4 asks for
  ({{ gib(spec(M1, "mem_total_bytes")) }} and {{ gib(spec(M3, "mem_total_bytes")) }} GiB of
  `MemTotal`, section 10.2), and the default no longer rests on M1 alone; like
  every verdict here it stays provisional until the pre-registration is frozen. The
  reservation that passes on all three machines is 128 KiB, which runs only
  {{ med(M1, DR, "D(B)/128K", "naive_depth_reached", "", 0) }}
  levels of the small recursion and
  {{ med(M1, DR, "D(B)/128K", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0) }} of the parser (M1;
  {{ med(M2, DR, "D(B)/128K", "naive_depth_reached", "", 0) }} and
  {{ med(M2, DR, "D(B)/128K", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0) }} on M2). This does not
  change how the guard is decided: section 2.4 trips it only when an ordinary
  program needs a default above 8 MiB that then fails the memory primary. 8 MiB
  is admissible on memory on M1 and M3 (it completes the 10k-deep parse on M1
  and M2) and is the step to take if the corpus histogram finds ordinary
  programs deeper than 1 MiB; above 8 MiB, memory must be re-measured (the
  plan's exhaustion row).
* **Probes, not guard pages.** One guard page per task stops at
  {{ med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks on M1 and
  {{ med(M2, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} on M2 under the default
  `vm.max_map_count`, and a guard-page trap discards the exhausted task's frames
  without running their destructors
  ({{ med(M1, DR, "A/1M", "naive_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0) }} drop guards skipped in
  the 1M-deep recursion at 1 MiB, on both machines; none under probes). With
  probes every exhaustion unwinds cleanly. The plan attaches a condition to
  this choice, probe overhead of at most 3 % on compute loops, and that
  condition is open: policy 1 leaves it to the native era, and at face value
  `fib` exceeds it on both machines, `tree` on M1 and the back-edge check on M2
  (section 7). Two further conditions come with probes: the runtime must
  resolve the unwinder's lazily bound symbols before tasks run (or link them
  eagerly), which this phase found and fixed (section 10.3); and the native
  compiler's probe should compare the stack pointer minus the frame size
  before allocating the frame, so that the reserve covers only the unwinder and
  unprobed callees. With both, the policy's 5 KiB (5,120-byte) reserve leaves
  {{ f"{5120 - max(reserve_max(M1), reserve_max(M2)):,}" }} bytes over the largest
  need measured on either machine.
* **`spawn` stays ambient**, without a capability. The research found no
  supply-chain attack that gating `spawn` would have stopped, and nothing in the
  harness argues otherwise: a task costs
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us (M1) and
  {{ rng(M2, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us (M2) to spawn and join and
  {{ im(M1, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} KiB at rest (M1 at 1M tasks; M3:
  {{ im(M3, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} KiB at 1M; M2: {{ im(M2, "D(B)/1M", 100000) }} KiB at
  100k), and the risk of code that spawns too
  many is a resource limit for the runtime to enforce (the scale row's guards),
  not an authority to grant.
* **Yield checks (Q-34)**, decided as compiler-inserted, need an interval and a
  placement. Yielding every 100k iterations of the test loop (slices of about
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us on M1
  and {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us on
  M2) held the probe's p99 at
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us, and
  every 10k at {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} and
  {{ rng(M2, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us. The switches cost
  little at those intervals: median throughput changes of
  {{ chg(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  and {{ chg(M1, ST, "B/1M", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} % on M1,
  within its five runs' spread, and
  {{ chg(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  and {{ chg(M2, ST, "B/1M", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} % on M2,
  which resolves them; every 1k iterations cost
  {{ loss(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  of the throughput on M1 and
  {{ loss(M2, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec") }} %
  on M2. The check itself is not free everywhere: on every back-edge it cost
  nothing measurable on M1 and
  {{ rng(M2, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % on M2 (section 7),
  more than the whole budget. A slice of a few hundred microseconds meets the
  starvation row by two orders of magnitude, so the compiler can afford to
  check far less often than every back-edge; how often, and where (after
  unrolling or vectorisation, or behind a counter), should be chosen to fit what
  the probes leave of the 3 % budget, which the native era measures.
* **The stackless fallback is not needed** on this evidence: the default
  strategy passes the memory primary with a wide margin (at 128 KiB on all
  three machines, at every reservation on M1 and M3, the 1 MiB default
  included), and every exhaustion is clean on both machines that ran the
  recursion. Its cost stays on file
  ([`stackless-fallback-estimate.md`](stackless-fallback-estimate.md): 17-21
  engineer-weeks, plus 3 if frames become values), for the case that would call
  for it: the corpus histogram (week 40) finding ordinary depth beyond 8 MiB.
  The other case, a 16 GiB second machine contradicting M1's 1M cells at the
  default reservation, did not arise: M3's shallow ratios at 1M are within
  {{ mem_spread(["B/128K", "B/1M", "B/8M", "C/36K", "C/40K", "C/48K", "D(B)/128K", "D(B)/1M", "D(B)/8M"], 1000000, (M1, M3), ("shallow",)) }}
  of M1's, and D(B)/1 MiB's worst ratio is {{ w(M3, "D(B)/1M") }} there against
  {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} on M1. The yield-point frame-boundary question is therefore not flagged to
  Phase 3.

### 9.3 What the harness era cannot settle

* **The 3 % probe budget.** Neither machine has hardware counters, M1's
  run-to-run noise exceeds the bar, and the two machines' figures for `fib` and
  for the back-edge check differ by more than the bar (section 7). The decision waits for the native era on bare metal.
* **The default reservation against real programs.** The harness shows what each
  size costs and how deep it goes; whether ordinary bet-1 and bet-2 programs fit
  1 MiB is the interpreter era's histogram.
* **Sisu's own frames.** Every number here is Rust code on native stacks. Frame
  sizes, where the compiler places probes and yield checks (before allocating a
  frame; on which back-edges; before or after vectorisation), how the unwinder
  or the runtime's own unwinding behaves, and what a Sisu task's record costs
  change these figures. The native era reruns this harness on Sisu frames, and
  only then is Q-6 closed finally (18: "closed provisionally with the harness
  era label ..., and finally when the native backend reruns the harness on Sisu
  frames").
* **How long a cancelled call may wait.** When a cancel cannot stop a read
  (`-EALREADY`), D-058 makes the call wait for the read's own completion, which
  keeps the buffer safe and can take as long as the I/O (section 8). Bounding
  that wait (re-issuing the cancel, poll-armed reads for pollable files) is a
  design question for the native runtime that the harness only observed.
* **The registered echo and connection cells.** The 10k-connection echo and
  the 100k idle connections ran on M2 only (M1's descriptor limit), and the
  open-loop load generator on disjoint cores of section 6.5 on neither machine.

## 10. Deviations, amendments, gaps

### 10.1 What the owner's policies changed relative to the plan

The plan (18, Phase 1) asked for "a report with every configuration, not the
best one, reproduced on a second machine by the reviewer", and its threshold
row for probe overhead presupposes machines with hardware counters and a fixed
frequency; the pre-registration's section 4.4 asks for two machines with at
least 16 GiB, default sysctls, the scale test in a 16 GiB memory cgroup, and M2
run by the reviewer. The owner's decisions of October 2026 (README, "Policy
decisions") changed that, and this report follows them:

| Policy | The plan's original wording | What this report does instead |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Machines | two machines, the second the reviewer's, implicitly bare metal | M1 is a 4-vCPU KVM virtual machine without a PMU and with an unpinned frequency; M2 is a GitHub-hosted runner (2 vCPUs on one core, {{ gib(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes"), 2) }} GiB, a Hyper-V guest without a PMU), driven by a workflow rather than by a reviewer; M3, for the memory benchmark only, is another GitHub-hosted runner ({{ spec(M3, "cpus_available") }} vCPUs, {{ gib(spec(M3, "mem_total_bytes")) }} GiB, a Hyper-V guest), driven by a second workflow. The 3 % probe bar is reported with its noise and not decided (section 7) |
| 2. Context switch | "an assembly context switch from an existing library" | corosensei 0.3.x, unchanged since the harness was built |
| 3. Sysctls and limits | default sysctls; the earlier full-scale script needed `RLIMIT_NOFILE` of 200,064 | default sysctls on every machine; on M2 and M3 the workflows restore the kernel default `vm.max_map_count=65530`, which M2's runner image raises to {{ spec("smoke", "sysctl.vm.max_map_count") }}. The 10k echo and the 100k idle connections run only where the hard limit allows: on M1 ({{ num(spec(M1, "rlimit_nofile").split("/")[1]) }}) the 10k echo is a failed row and idle connections ran at 9,900; on M2 ({{ num(spec(M2, "rlimit_nofile").split("/")[1]) }} inside the workflow's scope, section 10.2) both ran |
| 4. Go after the excursion | "within 4x Go", the Go figure unspecified | Go after four `runtime.GC()` and `FreeOSMemory` |
| 5. Default strategy | none named | D(B) |
| 6. Probe reserve | the harness defaults of 16 KiB (B) and 8 KiB (C) | 5 KiB for B, C and D(B), now the harness default (version 0.2.0) |
| 7. 1M tasks after the excursion | every strategy | only D(A) and D(B); A, B, C and Go measure the shallow phase at 1M (`--skip-excursion`) on every machine, and their cell reads "not run (policy 7)" |
| 8. Yield points | compute tasks "that yield every K iterations", Go with or without `Gosched` | compiler-inserted yield points every K = 10k and 100k iterations (and 1k); the baseline is Go with its default asynchronous preemption only; no yield points at all is reported, not counted |

### 10.2 Deviations from the harness protocol (proposal/20, section 6.5)

* **Machine 2's run, and the runs lost before it.** M2's results are the
  `harness-full` workflow's run 38518035636: one invocation of `run_all.sh
  --scale full` from {{ ver(M2, "date_utc") }}, five repetitions of the whole suite
  in rep-major order, without the quiet gate, inside the memory scope of
  section 0. Two earlier full-scale attempts were lost (section 0): 38495423545,
  whose artifact was never uploaded, and 38513665561, which starved the runner
  agent. The first one's log summary is a cross-check, not data of record, and
  it agrees with this run where the two should agree: D(B)/1 MiB's 100k
  ratios were {{ lost("memory rule", "D(B)/1M", 1) }} and
  {{ lost("memory rule", "D(B)/1M", 2) }} there
  ({{ imr(M2, "D(B)/1M", 100000) }} and {{ imr(M2, "D(B)/1M", 100000, "excursion") }}
  here), D(B)/128 KiB passed with w = {{ lost("memory rule", "D(B)/128K", 5, r"w = ([\d.]+)") }},
  the memory guard stopped D(B)/1 MiB at 1M after
  {{ lost("memory rule", "D(B)/1M", 3, r"at ([\d,]+) of") }} tasks, A stopped at
  {{ lost("max-tasks", "A/1M N=1000000", 1) }} tasks, and A1's runs on the five
  stackful strategies wrote
  {{ f"{sum(int(lost('cancel-uring', x + ' pipe_mix=cancel', 9, r'(\d[\d,]*) total').replace(',', '')) for x in STACKFUL_1M):,}" }}
  bytes after return. Its timing figures differ (B's spawn-join
  {{ lost("spawn-join", "B/1M", 3) }}x Go there, {{ rat(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x
  here; `fib`'s probe overhead {{ lost("probe-overhead", "B/1M", 1) }} % there,
  {{ med(M2, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % here), and the summary
  does not record the machine, so it cannot say whether the runner's processor
  was the same.
* **M2 is smaller than the plan's machine.** Section 4.4 asks for at least
  16 GiB and the scale test in a 16 GiB memory cgroup; M2 has
  {{ num(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes")) }} bytes ({{ gib(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes"), 2) }} GiB)
  and {{ spec(M2, "cpus_available") }} vCPUs (`machine-spec.csv`), the size the
  workflow's comment gives for `ubuntu-latest` on a private repository. On it
  the memory guard stopped the 1M-task `idle-memory` cells of B and D(B) at
  1 MiB and 8 MiB (section 0), so section 2.4 is inconclusive on M2 for D(B) at
  those reservations; `max-tasks`'s 1M pooled tasks fit (section 3). The
  workflow's 12 GiB scope did not bind on this machine (section 0). M3 measured
  those cells (section 1).
* **A third machine, for one benchmark.** Section 4.4 has two machines, the
  second the reviewer's, on which every configuration is reproduced. M3 was
  added after both had run, by a workflow rather than by the reviewer, to
  measure the cells M2 could not hold, and it ran `idle-memory` alone (every
  cell of it, five runs each, with the Go and Java controls); section 2.4's
  two-machine rule is applied to M1 with each runner (section 1). M3 has
  {{ num(spec(M3, "mem_total_bytes")) }} bytes ({{ gib(spec(M3, "mem_total_bytes")) }} GiB)
  of `MemTotal`, as M1 has {{ gib(spec(M1, "mem_total_bytes")) }} GiB, and its runs
  had a {{ gib(spec(M3, "cgroup_memory_max")) }} GiB scope, not a 16 GiB cgroup on a
  machine of at least 16 GiB. It runs Linux {{ spec(M3, "uname").split()[1] }} on
  {{ spec(M3, "os") }}, where M2 ran {{ spec(M2, "uname").split()[1] }} on
  {{ spec(M2, "os") }}: the image behind `ubuntu-latest` changed between the two
  runs, and the processor too ({{ spec(M3, "cpu_model") }} against M2's
  {{ spec(M2, "cpu_model") }}). M3's processor differs from M1's in vendor, as
  section 4.4 asks of the two machines where the available runners allow it.
  Its controls are {{ gover(M3) }} and JDK
  {{ jdk(M3).split()[0] }}.
* **M2's descriptor limit.** Inside the scope the processes had an
  `RLIMIT_NOFILE` of {{ spec(M2, "rlimit_nofile") }}; the runner's own processes had
  {{ spec("smoke", "rlimit_nofile") }} in the smoke run, and the lost run
  38495423545, outside any scope, ran idle connections at
  {{ f"{int([lab for lab in lost_labels('idle-connections') if lab.startswith('go ')][0].split('connections=')[1]):,}" }},
  the most (in whole hundreds) that a hard limit of
  {{ num(spec("smoke", "rlimit_nofile").split("/")[1]) }} allows. The run does not
  record why the scope's limit was higher; it is most likely inherited through
  `sudo`, which starts the scope. M3's scope, started by the same command on a
  later runner image ({{ spec(M3, "os") }}), had {{ spec(M3, "rlimit_nofile") }}, so
  the command alone does not set it. The workflow did not raise the limit, but its
  benchmarks did not run under the runner's default either. Under that default
  the 10k echo would still have run (it needs 20,064), and the 100k idle
  connections would have run at
  {{ f"{int([lab for lab in lost_labels('idle-connections') if lab.startswith('go ')][0].split('connections=')[1]):,}" }}.
  Neither cell decides a verdict: the echo row is not shown, and idle RSS is
  reported only.
* **M2's kernel and other limits.** M2 runs Linux
  {{ spec(M2, "uname").split()[1] }} with io_uring enabled, which meets section 4.4's
  "6.1 or later"; the workflow restores `vm.max_map_count` to
  {{ spec(M2, "sysctl.vm.max_map_count") }} (the image's is
  {{ spec("smoke", "sysctl.vm.max_map_count") }}), so A and D(A) meet the same wall as on
  M1. The scope's pids cgroup ({{ spec(M2, "cgroup_pids_max") }} tasks) stops OS threads
  at {{ med(M2, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }}, below M1's
  {{ med(M1, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} (where the mapping limit
  stops them first) and below the 10k OS threads of `idle-memory`, whose M2 cell is
  a failed row. The descriptor limit stopped no cell on M2: the A and D(A)
  idle-connection failures at 100k come, by the harness's code path, from the
  mapping limit (section 4). Hardware counters are unavailable there too
  ("{{ note(M2, PO, "B/1M", "instruction_counts") }}").
* **M2's huge pages and swap.** The workflow sets transparent huge pages to
  `madvise`, as on M1, and turns swap off. Under the image's `always`, Go's
  goroutines held {{ smoke_im("go", 10000, "excursion") }} KiB each after the
  excursion in the smoke run (before its GC), which on M2's memory would push
  Go's 100k cell into the memory guard.
* **M1's memory limit** is the sandbox's memory cgroup of
  {{ gib(spec(M1, "cgroup_memory_max")) }} GiB on a {{ gib(spec(M1, "mem_total_bytes")) }} GiB
  VM, not a 16 GiB cgroup on a machine of at least 16 GiB.
* **M1 is shared.** Other development work (compilations and tests elsewhere in
  the repository) ran on the VM during the runs, at times that were not
  recorded; section 6.5 assumes a machine that runs nothing else. The first
  runs of the four groups that measure time (spawn-join, starvation,
  probe-overhead, echo) were exposed to it: a listing of the compiler
  workspace's build directory made during the echo group showed files written
  in every ten-minute interval from 21:30 to 22:09 and from 22:20 to 22:43 UTC,
  which covers parts of the starvation, probe-overhead and echo groups (nothing
  is known about spawn-join's half minute at 19:54), and in the starvation
  group the first of B's five runs at K = 10k reached
  {{ num(min(float(r['value']) for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-before-quiet-gate/starvation.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'compute_iterations_per_sec' and r['strategy'] == 'B' and 'yield_every=10000;' in r['params'])) }}
  iterations per second against a median of
  {{ num(__import__('statistics').median(float(r['value']) for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-before-quiet-gate/starvation.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'compute_iterations_per_sec' and r['strategy'] == 'B' and 'yield_every=10000;' in r['params'])) }}.
  Those four groups were run again with the quiet gate of `load.sh`
  (`--quiet-below 0.3`, section 11), and only the gated runs are reported; the
  first runs are kept in `superseded-before-quiet-gate/`. The memory, scale and
  correctness groups (deep-recursion, idle-memory, max-tasks, cancel-uring) were
  not run again: what they report does not depend on other processes' CPU use,
  apart from incidental timings (cancellation latencies, spawn rates) that are
  not verdict inputs. A first gated run of spawn-join was stopped after two
  repetitions because `load.sh` then left interrupt and softirq time out of the
  machine's busy time, which this kernel's tick accounting charges to the
  interrupted process, so a competing process would have been invisible during
  the echo benchmark; its rows are in `aborted-gated-rerun/`. The gated runs
  changed no verdict: B's spawn-join median is
  {{ rat(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }} times Go's (first runs:
  {{ srat(SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}), B's starvation p99 at K = 10k
  {{ med(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us (first runs:
  {{ smed(ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }}), B's `fib` and `tree` probe
  overheads {{ med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % and
  {{ med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % (first runs:
  {{ smed(PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} and
  {{ smed(PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }}), and D(B)'s echo p99 at
  1000 connections {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}
  times Go's (first runs:
  {{ srat(EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}). What moved
  most is the throughput cost of yield points every 10k iterations,
  {{ f"{(1 - cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec')) * 100:.1f}" }} %
  against {{ f"{(1 - scell(ST, 'B/1M').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / scell(ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec')) * 100:.1f}" }} %
  in the first runs (section 6). M2 is a hosted runner that runs nothing but the
  job, measured without the gate: during its kept processes the other processes
  used a median of at most {{ load_median_max(M2) }} CPUs per benchmark (section
  11). {{ len(busy(M2)) }} of its {{ len(load_all(M2)) }} processes saw them use
  0.3 CPUs or more, none of them longer than
  {{ f"{max(float(r['wall_s']) for r in busy(M2)):.2f}" }} s:
  {{ len(busy(M2, bench=SJ)) }} in spawn-join (the pooled strategies' cells, section 2),
  {{ len(busy(M2, bench=DR)) }} in deep-recursion and {{ len(busy(M2, bench=MT)) }} in
  max-tasks, whose figures do not depend on CPU time. M3, also a hosted runner
  measured without the gate, saw the other processes use 0.3 CPUs or more
  during {{ len(busy(M3)) }} of its {{ len(load_all(M3)) }} processes, none of them longer
  than {{ f"{max(float(r['wall_s']) for r in busy(M3)):.2f}" }} s, in a benchmark whose
  figures do not depend on CPU time either.
* **Workers** are the VMs' vCPUs: 4 on M1, which the guest sees as four
  single-thread cores, 2 on M2, which it sees as one core's two hardware
  threads, and 4 on M3, which it sees as two cores with two hardware threads
  each. Section 4.4's default is one worker per physical core; on M2 that
  would be one, on M3 two.
* **Memory** is resident plus page tables (VmRSS + VmPTE from
  `/proc/self/status`), the harness's figure of record since its first
  version; section 6.5 says "resident set" from `/proc/self/smaps_rollup`.
  The RSS-only figures are in the CSVs and in `summarize.py`'s output, and
  every verdict is the same on them. The harness reads its baseline after
  runtime start-up without the 1,000-task warm-up of section 6.5; at most
  the first 1,000 tasks could find warm memory, so the effect is at most
  1 % at 100k and conservative (it raises the per-task figure) for every
  implementation. Section 6.5 also has the controls run a full collection
  before every reading; the Go control collects before its baseline and, for
  its after-excursion figure of record, four times after the excursion
  (policy 4), but reads its shallow figure without one. Its goroutines' stacks
  are then at Go's minimum size, which a collection does not shrink:
  {{ num(min(cell(m, IM, f"go N={n}").rng("go_stack_inuse_per_task_shallow")[0] for m in (M1, M2, M3) for n in (100000, 1000000))) }}-{{ num(max(cell(m, IM, f"go N={n}").rng("go_stack_inuse_per_task_shallow")[1] for m in (M1, M2, M3) for n in (100000, 1000000))) }}
  bytes of stack in use per goroutine in every run on every machine, at 100k
  and at 1M (`go_stack_inuse_per_task_shallow`).
* **Echo** is a closed loop with the clients in the same process and on the
  same vCPUs, measured for 30 s after 5 s of warm-up; section 6.5 asks for an
  open-loop load generator on disjoint cores at a fixed rate, 60 s after 10 s
  of warm-up, at 10k connections. M2's 10k figures and both machines'
  1000-connection figures are the harness's comparison of the runtimes, not the
  registered measurement.
* **Starvation** runs four compute tasks per worker as section 6.5 asks (16 on
  M1, 8 on M2), for 11 s, which gives each taskbench run about 10k wake-ups;
  Go's probe wakes much later under load, so its runs have fewer wake-ups
  (section 6 lists them).
* **Probe overhead** is 31 alternating repetitions of every variant inside
  one process, five processes per cell; section 6.5 asks for ten runs of
  each. The `loop` workload (the yield check on a back-edge) is new and is
  not in section 6.5's list.
* **A1** reads into a 4 KiB buffer on the task's stack from a pool of pipes
  that hold no data, cancels every read through its task handle, and checks
  the buffer twice: when the call returns, and again after enough "late"
  data has been written into every pipe for any read the kernel still held to
  complete, with the frame kept alive (and so never reused) until its batch
  of 8,192 ends. Section 6.5 writes a canary after the return and quarantines
  the memory; keeping the frame alive is the same check. "Completions that
  arrived after the call returned" cannot happen by construction in the safe
  API (`read` returns only on the completion) and are counted only in the
  negative control's `abandoned_reads`.
* **Controls.** Go is {{ trio(gover(M1), gover(M2), gover(M3)) }}, not the
  latest stable release; the JDK is 21.0.12 on M1 and M2 (Ubuntu's build on M1,
  Temurin's on M2) and Temurin's {{ jdk(M3).split()[0] }} on M3 (JEP 491's
  unpinning is 24 and later). The "C or Rust stackful
  coroutine library" control is strategy A itself (corosensei's own default
  stack is a guarded mapping per coroutine), not a separate library.
* **Reviewer.** No Go/Java reviewer has configured or checked the controls, M2
  and M3 were run by workflows rather than by the reviewer, and no referee has
  applied the thresholds; the verdicts are the runtime engineer's reading of the
  draft rules.

### 10.3 Changes to the harness in this phase (version 0.2.0)

* **Policy defaults.** The probe reserve is 5 KiB for B, C and D(B)
  (`stack::DEFAULT_PROBE_RESERVE`); version 0.2.0 marks every CSV's
  `machine-spec.csv` (`harness_version`).
* **Bug: the io_uring ring at full scale.** `--concurrency 8192` asked the
  kernel for a completion queue of 131,072 entries, above its maximum of
  65,536, so every full-scale `cancel-uring` run would have been a "skipped:
  io_uring unavailable" row. The ring sizes are now clamped to the kernel's
  limits (`rt::uring::ring_sizes`, with unit tests).
* **Bug: the first unwind ran the dynamic linker inside the probe reserve.**
  `libgcc_s` binds its calls into the C library lazily, and the resolver saves
  the full extended register state on the current stack: about 3 KiB on M1's
  AVX-512 processor. The first stack exhaustion of every process therefore
  needed about 5 KiB of reserve and later ones about 1.8-2.1 KiB; the earlier
  "5 KiB when landing pads run drop code" was this one-time cost, not drop
  code (with `LD_BIND_NOW=1` the first unwind also needs 1.8 KiB). M2's smoke
  run, made before the fix, shows the same pattern with a smaller register
  state: {{ smoke_vmax(DR, "B/1M", "parse_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} bytes for the
  first unwind and {{ smoke_vmax(DR, "B/1M", "naive_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} for the
  next. With the 5 KiB default, the unit test
  `exhaustion_is_a_clean_task_failure_on_every_strategy` overran a B slot's
  reserve, and its canary caught it. The runtime now resolves those bindings
  once, on an ordinary stack, before it starts its workers
  (`stack::probe::warm_unwinder`).
* **Bug: A1's at-return check.** The check expected a completed read to hold
  the round's data byte, but a read that a cancel could not stop (`-EALREADY`)
  legitimately completes with the late data the harness writes at the round's
  deadline, and returns after it. The first full-scale `cancel-uring` group
  (five repetitions) therefore reported {{ f"{sum(int(r['value']) for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-cancel-uring-before-check-fix/cancel-uring.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'buffer_writes_after_return' and r['status'] == 'failed'):,}" }}
  bytes "after return" in {{ sum(1 for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-cancel-uring-before-check-fix/cancel-uring.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'buffer_writes_after_return' and r['status'] == 'failed') }}
  runs, all at return and none afterwards (`bad_late=0`), each in a run whose
  slowest cancellation took about 30 s. Late bytes within the length the read
  itself returned now count as data it delivered (`completed_with_late_data`),
  and the group was rerun. The superseded rows are kept in
  `results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-cancel-uring-before-check-fix/`,
  outside `summarize.py`'s reach. The fix changed only
  `src/bench/cancel_uring.rs`: `versions.txt` shows the sources' digest
  `789d610d...` for deep-recursion and idle-memory (and the superseded first
  runs of spawn-join and cancel-uring), `51e42157...` for max-tasks and the
  cancel-uring rerun (and the superseded first runs of starvation,
  probe-overhead and echo), and `6a1fedf5...` for the gated groups, whose
  sources differ from `51e42157...` only in the scripts (`load.sh`, `run_all.sh`,
  `controls/run_controls.sh`): their `versions.txt` entries record the binary's
  digest, `5893a273...`, which is the binary cargo built from `51e42157...` at
  20:50 UTC for max-tasks and did not rebuild afterwards. M2 built
  `6a1fedf5...` with its own compiler (section 0).
* **New measurements for the registered protocol.** `idle-memory
  --skip-excursion` (policy 7); `max-tasks --hold-secs 60` (section 6.5's
  60-second survival); `cancel-uring --pipe-mix cancel` with 4 KiB buffers and
  the cancel-to-return latency (A1 as registered); `deep-recursion --framed`
  (256-byte frames with drop code in every frame); the `loop` workload of
  `probe-overhead` (policy 8); the SMT state, threads per core and hypervisor in
  `machine-spec`. The Go and Java controls take `--skip-excursion` and
  `--hold-secs` too.
* **The machine's load** (`load.sh`, sourced by `run_all.sh` and the controls):
  every benchmark process gets a row in `load.csv` with its wall-clock and CPU
  time and the CPU time the other processes used meanwhile (`/proc/stat`'s busy
  time of all CPUs, interrupts and softirqs included, less the process's own),
  and `--quiet-below CPUS` makes each process wait for a quiet machine and
  measures it again when the others were busy while it ran (the last of three
  attempts is kept whatever its load; discarded attempts keep their `load.csv`
  row and lose their CSV rows). `summarize.py` prints the load table of
  section 11. The gate is off by default, and off in the M2 workflow.
* **Scripts.** `scale.sh` holds both scales' parameters for `run_all.sh` and the
  controls; `run_all.sh --reps N` (five at full scale) runs repetitions in
  rep-major order with each benchmark's controls right after it, and writes
  `timing.csv` and a record of every invocation in `versions.txt` (with a digest
  of the sources and of the binary); the controls run Go's starvation with its default preemption
  only, and record a connection count beyond the hard limit as a failed row.
  `summarize.py` reports medians and ranges over runs, bootstrap intervals, the
  policies' baselines and section 2.4's rule, on each machine and on each pair
  of machines, and gives the range of tasks created over a cell's runs when the
  memory guard stops it; `report/tables.py` puts the machines side by side in
  every table, the three of them in the memory tables. The report's scripts change no measurement and are outside the
  sources' digest.

### 10.4 Not run, and why

| Cell | Machine | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Echo at 10k connections | M1 | both ends in one process need 2 x 10,000 + 64 = 20,064 descriptors; the hard `RLIMIT_NOFILE` is {{ num(spec(M1, "rlimit_nofile").split("/")[1]) }} and cannot be raised (policy 3). A client in a second process would fit, and is not implemented |
| Idle connections at 100k | M1 | the same limit; run at 9,900, the most it allows |
| 1M tasks after the excursion for A, B, C and Go | M1, M2, M3 | policy 7; about 33 GiB |
| `idle-memory` at 1M tasks: B and D(B) at 1 MiB and 8 MiB | M2 | failed rows: the memory guard stopped them at {{ created(M2, "B/1M", 1000000) }} (B/1 MiB), {{ created(M2, "B/8M", 1000000) }} (B/8 MiB), {{ created(M2, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} (D(B)/1 MiB) and {{ created(M2, "D(B)/8M", 1000000) }} (D(B)/8 MiB) of 1,000,000 tasks, on a {{ gib(spec(M2, "mem_total_bytes"), 2) }} GiB machine; the 12 GiB scope did not bind (section 0). M3 measured them (section 1) |
| Every benchmark except `idle-memory` | M3 | the run was made for the million-task `idle-memory` cells that M2 could not hold (`run_all.sh --only idle-memory`, section 0); M1 and M2 ran every benchmark |
| Idle connections at 100k for A and D(A) | M2 | failed rows: the client's `connect` timed out in every run; a guarded mapping per server task meets the VMA limit near {{ med(M2, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks (section 4) |
| OS threads beyond {{ med(M2, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} (`idle-memory` at 10k, `max-tasks`) | M2 | the scope's pids cgroup of {{ spec(M2, "cgroup_pids_max") }} tasks: thread creation fails with `EAGAIN`, a failed row |
| Instruction counts for probe overhead | M1, M2 | no PMU on either machine |
| The semantics rows, the `sim` study, the corpus stack-depth histogram, interpreter starvation | every machine | interpreter era (Phases 6-9), not Phase 1 |

### 10.5 Measured on one machine only

On M1 only: OS threads beyond M2's pids limit in `max-tasks` (in
`idle-memory`, M1 and M3 ran the 10k OS threads that the limit stopped on M2).
On M2 only: the 10k-connection echo and idle connections at 100k (where A and
D(A) failed), which M1's descriptor limit excludes. The 1M-task `idle-memory`
cells of B and D(B) at 1 MiB and 8 MiB, which M2's memory guard stopped, were
measured on M1 and M3. Every other full-scale cell (100k and 1M tasks, five
runs, 1M cancelled reads, the 60-second hold, starvation with four tasks per
worker, the framed recursion, the back-edge yield check) was measured on M1 and
M2, five runs each, and every `idle-memory` cell on M3 as well.

## 11. How the runs were made

On M1, each benchmark group was one invocation, all into the same directory:

```
./run_all.sh --scale full --reps 5 --machine-id vm --out results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1 --only <benchmark> [--quiet-below 0.3]
```

M1 is a development VM, and other work (compilations and tests elsewhere in the
repository) ran on it at times that were not recorded. No two benchmark
processes ran at once. The groups ran in this order: deep-recursion,
spawn-join, idle-memory, cancel-uring, max-tasks, cancel-uring again (after the
fix of A1's check), starvation, probe-overhead and echo; then, because the
first runs of the four groups that measure time had shared the VM with that
work (section 10.2), spawn-join, probe-overhead, starvation and echo again,
and idle-connections, all five with `--quiet-below 0.3`: before every process
the script waited until the other processes had used fewer than 0.3 CPUs over
2 s, and it measured a process again (up to three attempts) when they had used
0.3 CPUs or more on average while it ran, and at least 0.05 CPU seconds in all
(five clock ticks, so that a process of a few milliseconds is not measured
again for one stray tick; `load.sh`). Within a group the
repetitions run in rep-major order (every configuration and its controls once,
then again). The idle-connections group's runner was stopped by a two-hour limit
on background jobs in the environment that ran it, during the fifth repetition
and after the harness's seven configurations; that repetition's Go and Java
runs were then made by hand with the same gate (`controls/run_controls.sh ...
--only idle-connections --no-build --quiet-below 0.3`, described in
`aborted-gated-rerun/why-aborted.txt`), so `timing.csv` has four repetitions of
that group and every cell has five runs. Wall-clock time per group, from the `timing.csv` files of the
results directory and its subdirectories:

{{ table("timing") }}

On M2, the workflow `.github/workflows/harness-full.yml` restored the kernel's
`vm.max_map_count`, turned swap off, set transparent huge pages to `madvise`,
installed JDK 21 and Go 1.24, built the harness, and ran one invocation for the
whole suite inside the memory scope of section 0:

```
./run_all.sh --scale full --machine-id github-runner --out results/github-runner/full-${GITHUB_RUN_ID}
```

The five repetitions run in rep-major order over the whole suite (every
benchmark and its controls once, then again), without the quiet gate, since a
hosted runner runs nothing but the job. Wall-clock time per group, from its
`timing.csv`:

{{ table("timing_m2") }}

On M3, the workflow `.github/workflows/harness-cell.yml` prepared the runner as
M2's does, built the harness, and ran one invocation of the memory benchmark
inside its 14 GiB scope (section 0):

```
./run_all.sh --scale full --only idle-memory --machine-id github-runner-16g --out results/github-runner/cell-${GITHUB_RUN_ID}
```

The five repetitions run in rep-major order (every configuration and its
controls once, then again), without the quiet gate. Wall-clock time, from its
`timing.csv`:

{{ table("timing_m3") }}

The machine's load during every process of M1's gated groups and of every M2
and M3 group, from `load.csv` (the other processes' CPU time is all CPUs' busy
time from `/proc/stat` less the process's own; it can be a few ticks below
zero):

{{ table("load_summary") }}

The other work on M1 came in bursts: the gate measured a process again
{{ sum(1 for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/load.csv')) if r['kept'] == 'no') }}
times. Four processes were kept on their third attempt although the other
processes had used more than 0.3 CPUs while they ran (the last attempt is kept
whatever its load): two starvation runs at K = 10k (C and D(A)) and two echo
runs at 1000 connections (A and C). The two echo runs have the highest p99 of
their cells, and the two starvation runs lie within their cells' ranges, far
under the threshold; none of these cells decides a verdict. On M2 and M3 no
process was measured again (the gate was off); section 10.2 counts the
processes during which the other processes used 0.3 CPUs or more.
